+# BLOCKED: exact streaming median in O(1) memory
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`test_constant_memory` requires `median(feed())` over a **one-shot generator** of
+200,000 floats to:
+
+1. return the exact same value as the median computed from the fully sorted
+   sample, and
+2. peak below 64 KB of traced memory.
+
+Requirement 1 is met. Requirement 2 cannot be met by any correct implementation,
+so `test_constant_memory` still fails on the memory assertion.
+
+Measured with the test's own `feed()` (Python 3.15):
+
+```
+result:     120.12043743895683   # matches the sorted-based expected value
+peak bytes: 7,224,208            # limit is 65,536
+```
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+Exact selection on a stream has a known space lower bound: any deterministic
+one-pass algorithm that returns the exact k-th smallest element of an arbitrary
+sequence must retain Ω(n) information (Munro & Paterson, 1980; the result also
+holds for randomized algorithms with exact correctness). Intuitively, every
+element read could still turn out to be the median, so an exact algorithm
+cannot forget earlier elements.
+
+Specifics for this case:
+
+- The input is a **generator**, so it can be consumed only once. A multi-pass
+  threshold/bisection scheme (count `< t`, `> t`, `== t` per pass) can select an
+  exact double in O(1) memory, but only if the input can be re-iterated. It
+  cannot here.
+- Floating-point values have a 2^64-sized ordered domain (about 2^64 distinct
+  bit patterns), so an exact counting/bucket array is not constant memory
+  either.
+- Approximate sketches (Greenwald–Khanna, t-digest, reservoir sampling) use
+  memory independent of `n`, but return an estimate. They cannot satisfy the
+  test's exact `assertEqual(got, want)` (or the function's documented "exact
+  median" contract), so using one would trade away correctness, not solve the
+  requirement.
+
+These two requirements — exactness and O(1) memory on a single-pass iterator —
+are mutually exclusive, not just hard to combine.
+
+## Workarounds deliberately not taken
+
+The following would make the memory number pass without providing constant
+memory, and were rejected as dishonest workarounds:
+
+- spilling values to a temp file or other external storage (still O(n) storage,
+  just untraced);
+- using `numpy`/`array`/native buffers to hold the data outside `tracemalloc`'s
+  accounting (still O(n) memory);
+- exploiting the fixed RNG seed to regenerate the sample (test-specific, not a
+  median algorithm);
+- weakening, skipping, or patching the test/assertions.
+
+## What was fixed
+
+`median` returned the upper middle element for even-length input
+(`median([1, 2, 3, 4]) == 3`). It now returns the mean of the two middle
+elements (`2.5`), matching the docstring. `test_odd`, `test_even`,
+`test_empty_raises`, and `test_generator_input` pass.
+
+`median` remains exact, using O(n) time and memory (`sorted(values)`).
+
+## Options for infra
+
+To have a passing, honest test, pick one:
+
+1. **Relax exactness**: allow a documented approximation (e.g. Greenwald–Khanna
+   with an explicit ε) and assert within a tolerance. Memory can then be
+   bounded by ε, not `n`.
+2. **Allow external storage**: explicitly permit disk spill / external
+   selection and test memory+disk behavior rather than traced memory alone.
+3. **Allow re-iteration**: pass a re-iterable sequence (or a factory) and use
+   the O(1)-memory, multi-pass counting selection described above.
+4. **Accept O(n) memory**: drop the constant-memory requirement and bound
+   per-worker chunk size or memory elsewhere.
+
+Until one of these is chosen, `test_constant_memory` is expected to fail on
+line 38 (`assertLess(peak, 64 * 1024)`); its value assertion passes.
